"""Original marrow-failure questions: acquired and inherited aplasia."""
from qbank_authoring import question,ref,finalize
AA=ref('ASH: Severe and Very Severe Acquired Aplastic Anemia Guidelines, 2026','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13546908/')
BS=ref('BSH: Adult Aplastic Anaemia Guideline, 2024','https://cms.b-s-h.org.uk/guidelines/guidelines/guidelines-for-the-diagnosis-and-management-of-adult-aplastic-anaemia')
FA=ref('GeneReviews: Fanconi Anemia','https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK1401/')
EB=ref('EBMT Handbook: Acquired Marrow Failure—Severe AA and PNH','https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608280/')
rows=[]
def q(*a,**k):rows.append(question(*a,**k))
q('An adult has pancytopenia, very low reticulocytes and a markedly hypocellular marrow without an infiltrate or increased blasts. Which diagnosis is a leading consideration after exclusion of mimics?', 'Aplastic anemia',[
 ('Pure red-cell aplasia','This primarily affects the erythroid lineage rather than all three.'),('Peripheral hypersplenism as the best fit','The markedly hypocellular marrow points to production failure.'),('Acute leukemia established without blasts','The provided marrow pattern does not establish leukemia.')],
 'Aplastic anemia is a marrow-production failure syndrome', '**Pancytopenia with hypocellularity is the hallmark.**',['Exclude hypoplastic MDS and inherited marrow-failure syndromes.','Drug, toxin and infection history can matter.'], '**Classify severity and begin supportive care.**',['Neutropenic infection and bleeding risks can be immediate.','Early donor and treatment assessment should proceed in parallel.'],[['Blood','Pancytopenia and low reticulocytes'],['Marrow','Marked hypocellularity'],['Task','Exclude mimics and assess severity']],[AA,BS],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with confirmed aplastic anemia has marrow cellularity 10%, ANC 0.12 × 10^9/L, platelets 12 × 10^9/L and profoundly reduced reticulocytes. Which severity classification fits?', 'Very severe aplastic anemia',[
 ('Nonsevere aplastic anemia','The cytopenias meet severe criteria and the ANC is below the very-severe threshold.'),('Severe AA but not very severe','An ANC below 0.2 × 10^9/L defines the additional very-severe category when severe-AA criteria are met.'),('Pure red-cell aplasia','The neutrophil and platelet abnormalities are substantial.')],
 'Severity combines marrow cellularity and peripheral counts', '**Very severe AA meets severe-AA criteria with ANC below 0.2 × 10^9/L.**',['The provided marrow and multiple counts meet the severe framework.','Use the correct reticulocyte method and thresholds when applying a formal criteria set.'], '**The profound neutropenia increases urgency.**',['Investigate and treat fever promptly.','Coordinate definitive treatment and supportive care.'],[['Cellularity','Markedly reduced'],['Counts','Multiple severe cytopenias'],['ANC','Below 0.2: very severe category']],[AA,BS],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which feature should prompt a careful reconsideration of uncomplicated acquired aplastic anemia?', 'Marked splenomegaly with a leukoerythroblastic smear',[
 ('Low reticulocytes','This is compatible with marrow failure.'),('Hypocellular marrow without infiltration','This supports an aplastic pattern in context.'),('Thrombocytopenia and neutropenia','These are expected lineage abnormalities.')],
 'A production-failure label must fit the anatomy and smear', '**Marked organomegaly and leukoerythroblastosis raise alternative infiltrative, fibrotic or other processes.**',['Review the trephine architecture.','Look for infection, malignancy and splenic mechanisms as appropriate.'], '**Do not force discordant findings into the initial diagnosis.**',['Aplasia and another disorder can coexist.','Use integrated morphology, genetics and clinical assessment.'],[['Typical AA','Hypocellular production failure'],['Discordant clue','Marked spleen and immature circulating cells'],['Response','Reassess the differential']],[BS,EB])
q('Why are inherited marrow-failure syndromes assessed before finalizing treatment for apparent acquired aplastic anemia?', 'They can alter conditioning tolerance, donor selection, cancer risk and the treatment strategy',[
 ('They can always be excluded by normal adult appearance','Some present without classic physical findings.'),('They have no implications for related donors','A related donor can share a relevant germline defect.'),('They are diagnosed solely by the degree of thrombocytopenia','Specific clinical and laboratory assessment is needed.')],
 'The same marrow phenotype can arise from different biological diseases', '**Germline disease can present in adulthood.**',['Family history, congenital findings and organ disease provide clues.','Telomere and chromosome-instability pathways can affect treatment risk.'], '**Select confirmatory testing and donor assessment appropriately.**',['Use genetics expertise when indicated.','Do not assume a standard conditioning regimen is safe for every inherited syndrome.'],[['Diagnosis','Acquired versus inherited mechanism'],['Donor','Related-donor implications'],['Treatment','Conditioning and toxicity differences']],[AA,FA])
q('Short stature, radial-ray anomalies, pigmentary skin changes and marrow failure raise suspicion for Fanconi anemia. Which test is classically used?', 'Chromosome-breakage testing with diepoxybutane or mitomycin C, with appropriate genetic evaluation',[
 ('Ham acidified-serum test','This is an obsolete PNH-related assay, not the Fanconi test.'),('Eosin-5-maleimide binding alone','That evaluates a red-cell membrane phenotype.'),('Direct antiglobulin testing alone','That addresses immune red-cell coating.')],
 'Fanconi anemia is a DNA crosslink-repair disorder', '**Cells show increased chromosome breakage under appropriate crosslinking stress.**',['Molecular testing can identify the responsible gene.','Somatic mosaicism can complicate the choice and interpretation of samples.'], '**Assess more than the current cytopenias.**',['Malignancy risk and treatment sensitivity matter.','Genetic counseling and family/donor assessment are important.'],[['Pathway','DNA crosslink repair'],['Test','DEB / MMC chromosome breakage'],['Implication','Marrow failure and cancer predisposition']],[FA],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with marrow failure has a family history of pulmonary fibrosis and cirrhosis, with premature hair graying. Which inherited mechanism deserves assessment?', 'A telomere biology disorder',[
 ('An isolated acquired iron deficiency as the full explanation','It does not integrate the multisystem family pattern.'),('A red-cell-only membrane defect','That does not typically explain familial marrow, lung and liver disease.'),('An ABO incompatibility present since birth','This is not the described inherited multisystem mechanism.')],
 'A shared maintenance pathway can affect marrow, lung and liver', '**Short-telomere syndromes can present as adult organ disease without a classic childhood syndrome.**',['The family history is informative.','Appropriate telomere and germline testing requires expert interpretation.'], '**Adapt treatment planning to the biological risk.**',['Conditioning and organ toxicity may be increased.','Related donor evaluation can require additional care.'],[['Organs','Marrow, lung and liver'],['Clue','Familial pattern and early graying'],['Implication','Inherited-failure and transplant assessment']],[AA,EB],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A fit young adult has severe acquired AA and a suitable matched sibling donor. Which treatment is an important first-line definitive option?', 'Allogeneic hematopoietic stem-cell transplantation',[
 ('Corticosteroid monotherapy as the usual curative strategy','This is not standard definitive treatment for severe acquired AA.'),('Splenectomy as the routine correction of hypocellular marrow','Removing the spleen does not replace deficient marrow hematopoiesis.'),('Iron alone despite normal stores','This does not correct the stem-cell failure.')],
 'Definitive treatment depends on age, fitness, donor and disease factors', '**Matched-sibling transplantation is a major first-line option in suitable severe AA.**',['The balance against IST is individualized.','Inherited failure must be considered before donor and conditioning decisions.'], '**Discuss benefits and transplant-specific harms.**',['Engraftment, infection, GVHD and fertility risks matter.','Continue transfusion and infection support during planning.'],[['Selection','Fit patient and appropriate donor'],['Option','Allogeneic HSCT'],['Trade-off','Definitive potential with transplant risks']],[AA,BS],style='clinical_scenario')
q('An adult with severe acquired AA is selected for first-line immunosuppressive treatment rather than immediate transplant. Which is a standard modern regimen?', 'Horse ATG plus ciclosporin with eltrombopag in an appropriate patient',[
 ('Prednisone alone indefinitely','This is not the usual effective definitive IST combination.'),('Rituximab alone for every case','B-cell depletion is not the standard first-line AA regimen.'),('Hydroxyurea to suppress all residual marrow activity','This is not an aplastic-anemia treatment strategy.')],
 'Immune suppression and stem-cell stimulation can be complementary', '**Horse ATG and ciclosporin address immune-mediated marrow suppression.**',['Eltrombopag can improve hematological response in the appropriate regimen.','Patient eligibility and local access affect implementation.'], '**Monitor response and treatment toxicity.**',['Renal, hepatic and infection-related complications need attention.','Follow for relapse and clonal evolution over time.'],[['ATG / ciclosporin','Immune-directed component'],['Eltrombopag','Thrombopoietin-receptor agonist component'],['Follow-up','Counts, toxicity and clonal surveillance']],[AA,BS])
q('Why does eltrombopag have a role in severe AA despite the presence of anemia and neutropenia as well as thrombocytopenia?', 'Thrombopoietin-receptor signaling can support early hematopoietic stem/progenitor cells and multilineage recovery',[
 ('It directly transfuses all three blood-cell lineages','It is a drug, not a cellular transfusion.'),('Its only possible effect is transient platelet aggregation','It is not simply a platelet-aggregation agent.'),('It removes every autoreactive T cell directly','That is not its principal mechanism.')],
 'A receptor associated with platelets also matters in early hematopoiesis', '**The effect can extend beyond the platelet lineage.**',['Responses require time and are not guaranteed.','It is integrated with a disease-specific treatment plan.'], '**Continue supportive care and monitoring during the response interval.**',['Review liver function and drug/food interactions according to the product.','Monitor marrow and blood changes for clonal evolution.'],[['Target','Thrombopoietin receptor'],['Level','Early stem/progenitor support'],['Outcome','Possible multilineage response']],[AA,BS])
q('A patient with severe AA develops fever with ANC 0.1 × 10^9/L. What is the priority?', 'Urgent assessment and empiric treatment of neutropenic infection under the local protocol',[
 ('Wait for a positive blood culture before antibiotics','Profound neutropenia makes delay dangerous.'),('Assume fever is harmless because the marrow is hypocellular','Marrow failure increases infection risk.'),('Treat only with oral iron','This does not address the infectious emergency.')],
 'Supportive care can be immediately life-saving while definitive treatment takes effect', '**Profound neutropenia and fever require rapid action.**',['Obtain cultures promptly without delaying indicated antimicrobials.','Assess for sepsis and relevant fungal risk.'], '**Coordinate infection and marrow-failure care.**',['Transfusion support and prophylaxis follow the individual risk plan.','Treatment response may be delayed, so supportive measures cannot be deferred.'],[['Risk','Profound neutropenia'],['Immediate care','Sepsis and infection pathway'],['Parallel care','Marrow-failure treatment and support']],[AA,BS],style='clinical_scenario')
q('After an initial response to AA immunosuppression, why is long-term follow-up still required?', 'Relapse, recurrent cytopenias, PNH expansion or clonal evolution can occur',[
 ('A normal CBC once proves permanent cure in every case','Late events remain possible.'),('Follow-up is needed only to repeat serum iron forever','The relevant risks extend beyond iron.'),('All responding patients inevitably develop leukemia','Risk is not the same as inevitability.')],
 'A response does not end the disease course', '**Track blood counts and symptoms over time.**',['Reassess new or progressive cytopenias.','Marrow and molecular reassessment may be needed when the pattern changes.'], '**Individualize surveillance and treatment adjustment.**',['Avoid abrupt unsupervised changes to maintenance immunosuppression.','Discuss the signs that should trigger earlier review.'],[['Potential event','Relapse'],['Potential event','PNH or clonal change'],['Follow-up','Counts and targeted reassessment']],[AA,BS])
q('Why is testing for a PNH clone relevant in acquired AA?', 'A GPI-deficient clone can coexist with immune marrow failure and inform characterization and follow-up',[
 ('Any detected clone automatically proves classical hemolytic PNH needing a drug','Small subclinical clones can occur without important hemolysis.'),('PNH testing replaces marrow assessment entirely','It answers a different part of the diagnostic question.'),('A clone means inherited Fanconi anemia is confirmed','These are distinct mechanisms.')],
 'AA and PNH can overlap without being identical clinical syndromes', '**A clone is a biological finding that needs a clinical interpretation.**',['Hemolysis, thrombosis and clone trajectory affect its significance.','The dominant problem may still be marrow underproduction.'], '**Treat the clinically relevant component.**',['Small nonhemolytic clones often need monitoring rather than immediate complement inhibition.','Significant hemolytic or thrombotic disease can change the plan.'],[['Clone','GPI-deficient cell population'],['AA phenotype','Production failure may dominate'],['Treatment','Based on actual hemolytic/thrombotic burden']],[AA,EB])
